#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572805} :activity/id #uuid "a69ca5e3-a84e-59d6-93a3-091c2895e2a2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049105 :topic/id #uuid "daf230f5-1a2c-50ef-8877-f60d57223e1e" :topic/math-academy-id 2084} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572742 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572702 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c1d31c7-1766-52ff-801d-c1018b5cf8ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/a7/a7fb57dc38c68d733c3d542d38c72535ba98282a15faec590ab258667969a3eb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572389} {:answer/id #uuid "76bbeca7-f809-59dc-96bc-cb9ef46fa3f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/6f/6f9f2ce252975ee7c375071a74256b1e3a8822fbea8c2787af20f566a495102a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572450} {:answer/id #uuid "9bc014c9-a389-5239-9d37-21c4b6a45e78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/5f/5f5dbab60c5fc142d370001eac4d7f3504e988d58f41a854b497b17d9ae31a9d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572481} {:answer/id #uuid "9c66d0b5-973e-532e-b772-7880b8902102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f6/f62a611bb3801a116885c625e98455768f8d2b7c14a61e6ff41e8f0c16c124e7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572482} {:answer/id #uuid "cfec009b-afc3-58ef-9bdd-50c4ae92d4de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ad/ad4d35c6082cd767fc770c0aaa784dd653294f3dad0361c16c31905299175912.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572549}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572450} :answer-field/id #uuid "13d53b8e-b395-5585-a8ae-f571e3f96687" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "88a63990-6594-5760-b73c-c8f3ff85a424" :question/math-academy-id "q-38949" :question/problem "Given that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+3x+3,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x+1)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 & 3 & 3 \\\\  &  & -1 & 0 & -3 \\\\  & 1 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(x)}{x+1}={x}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+1)({x}^{2}+3).\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of ${x}^{2}+3=0$ is \n\n$$\nD={0}^{2}-4(1)(3)=-12<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=-1.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={0}^{3}+{0}^{2}+3(0)+3=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,3),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-1,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/6f/6f9f2ce252975ee7c375071a74256b1e3a8822fbea8c2787af20f566a495102a.png)"} :step/id #uuid "27e75034-4701-5c22-a371-03bed6e619ad" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572806}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572805 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572668 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57886281-56d8-54cd-b5ee-42e3787e64c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b6/b648cf07daf5babeb4bcfc064a09e5ad18c72f1f8811121dc7a57ab6dd31b47a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572421} {:answer/id #uuid "a9bb3a61-8a73-5f70-964d-bf48a4acfd84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ce/cebfee355cc0ea19dbfe16a949269070792aa2b017637a78440f1c86d3c93e96.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572498} {:answer/id #uuid "cc99f072-396e-58d8-abba-10c6fdf37ceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/29/296e6670d8c9eff997f6f6f4e186e4b0219177cfa860a64a2b257cbc1e81c216.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572545} {:answer/id #uuid "cf099139-689e-5f75-a4dd-b68446c6bcde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/de/de0741fcb960e2b140660ac01307c8d6e69c3998fb79233ba8d17755c3d10fc0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572548} {:answer/id #uuid "f2efe1eb-3bf2-5ed9-a383-1cd9053f65ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/cf/cf4acc2efcee2c592809e993cdf68c937396320b42122fd8eeb5deb83b6c2619.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572580}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572548} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3431335-323c-5343-839e-0e15fdbf3890" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572637}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0d6ccda5-f064-5d51-bbf4-385a52f598a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-28194" :question/problem "Given that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-4x-2,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x+1)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -1 & -1 & -3 & -4 & -2 \\\\  &  & 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\  & -1 & -2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(x)}{x+1} & =-{x}^{2}-2x-2 \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x+2),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=-(x+1)({x}^{2}+2x+2).\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of ${x}^{2}+2x+2=0$ is \n\n$$\nD={2}^{2}-4(1)(2)=-4<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=-1.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\ny=-(0)^{3}-3(0)^{2}-4(0)-2=-2\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,-2),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-1,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph has an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/de/de0741fcb960e2b140660ac01307c8d6e69c3998fb79233ba8d17755c3d10fc0.png)"} :step/id #uuid "f38c45ec-f7e7-5e6d-bfaf-abc01579077f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572742}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572806 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572713 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22d3ed53-480e-5d14-b210-0937b5a16efb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/0c/0ca192db022a0c468fbbf3164b8e69ca0a6c836dcbda854fa681e254534fd23f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572363} {:answer/id #uuid "48acf1e2-58b9-5241-a083-38d03bb7ecb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/23/235421688cb2a0f7c2d8d2720dd508ea3f9dd6f4f149b152c3bd4441c6b218f2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572403} {:answer/id #uuid "5ab45d73-c28c-5b3d-af71-f611454a0422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b9/b9fbeec18a2cbaab7847c87822e1739e2759665c55b692bb4f0160285b8e1614.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572425} {:answer/id #uuid "a179156b-6c47-515a-b1a1-990c9e61b6d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f6/f621534a395f8b0692d31bb3b491b0237877e6dc3ea94a2299d354857c7c3bfd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572489} {:answer/id #uuid "c77677fb-023f-5df7-b72b-281a41661a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/1b/1bc6a076bab27bb8637412fcae42e0d778b558b43c10c4654293ad0e33d54a16.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572540}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572489} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6872c8f-cb1d-508e-9ef1-d93eedaf47d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bec25d7a-3c2c-5bfa-ad41-b6aabdf70b0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-39041" :question/problem "Given that $x$ is a factor of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-4x,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the polynomial. We factor out a common factor $-x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-4x \\\\  & =-x({x}^{2}+2x+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of ${x}^{2}+2x+4=0$ is \n\n$$\nD={2}^{2}-4(1)(4)=-12<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=0.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\ny=-{0}^{3}-2(0)^{2}-4(0)=0\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,0),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=0,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph has an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f6/f621534a395f8b0692d31bb3b491b0237877e6dc3ea94a2299d354857c7c3bfd.png)"} :step/id #uuid "f5acfea7-f360-5a2b-8c6f-494fe18b8b17"}] :activity/title "Graphing Cubic Curves Containing One Distinct Real Root" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572310}]}
